A||||6||Eric||Fiero wrapped around a pole||||4
Z||000000||Eric||10-10-1999||07:54 PM||||I was just watching the news and saw something that really caught my eye.  A news report of a Fiero that crashed this weekend here in Columbus.  There was a driver and passenger, both of whom survived.  The driver, who was a woman, drove the car square into a pole.  I bet that hurt!  The front of the car was gone and believe it or not, the passenger compartment looked relatively undamaged.  If I had to guess, I would say that the car was doing atleast 40 mph when it hit.  The wheels were perpendicular to the direction of the car.  It was kind of scary, but relieving at the same time.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Greg Piet||10-10-1999||09:28 PM||||While scanning through teh websites I saw somewhere that the fiero did extremely well in crash tests as far as driver safety.<BR>I think i saw on a site (dont know how accurate it is) that in 85 the fiero was rated #2 in crash tests losing to a volvo.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||10-10-1999||10:33 PM||||Greg, that was a Consumer Reports crash test study. The FOCOA magazine had an article in it a while back where a Fiero was dragged a long ways by a train and the driver not only survived, but made two phone calls on her cell phone. One to her mother and the other to 911. The picture of the mangled weckage was unbelieveable.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||10-11-1999||12:00 AM||||I saw the espisode about the girl and the train just a few weeks ago on 20/20 or simialr, maybe dateline.  Anyway, I never even realized that was a Fiero! It was so torn up and trashed, I couldn't believe she walked away from it.  Shows just how strong a Fiero is, they didn't take forever designing these cars for nothing. I still think they're very safe, even for todays standards.  And we don't even have airbags, abs, or other "safety" standards.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||10-11-1999||11:19 AM||||Just writting or should I say an article for the Golden Gate Fieros and ran across an article about safety on the internet. I decided to copy this article for the newsletter. It can be foun at the following address. <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/fieromag/articles/articles.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/fieromag/articles/articles.html</A> <BR>once you are in that page click on fiero safety ||||reg||14
Z||000005||RZURCTN||10-12-1999||12:59 PM||||I remember when I bounced my first Fiero off a guard rail.  (cattle guard got the best of my traction)  It took a pretty hard hit.  Damage to the car was minimal, tore up the bra and broke the turn signal.  The guard rail had a broken pole on it though.  I split it right down the middle! And they aren't little poles either. <BR>   The biggest fear I ever hear about is an engine compartment fire.  I can understand why.  The wires to the starter are always getting fried.  What can be done to prevent that?  I have only owned 2 84's. <BR>||||reg||12
Z||000006||theogre||10-12-1999||01:53 PM||||There are a number of things you can do to prevent wire cooking. Item # 1 is route them away from the exhaust. Get or make some clips to keep the wire down by the bottom of the block.<P>you can also get some fiberglass jacket for them but that won't help much if the wire is getting to close to the exhaust to start with.||||reg||1
